# Static analysis of the PHP backend.
#
# Why this exists: CodeQL scans only javascript-typescript and go (see
# .github/workflows/codeql.yml), and GitHub CodeQL has no PHP support at all -
# so the entire backend, where every authorisation, CSRF and token check lives,
# had no static analysis whatsoever. A security audit on 2026-08-23 found two
# PHP-side issues that CodeQL was structurally incapable of seeing.
#
# Level 0 is deliberate, not timid. It catches the class of bug this project
# actually shipped: badge.php called a function that did not exist and returned
# 500 for months, because `php -l` only checks syntax and nobody opened the
# page. Level 0 finds exactly that - unknown functions, classes, methods and
# wrong argument counts - and it reports ZERO false positives here, so the gate
# needs no baseline and every future failure is real.
#
# Higher levels are not used yet: level 1 adds ~550 "Variable $pdo might not be
# defined" reports, which are an artefact of this codebase's require-based
# wiring rather than defects. Raising the level would mean a large baseline,
# and a baseline is a list of problems marked "ignore" - the opposite of what
# this gate is for.
parameters:
    level: 0
    paths:
        - apps/status
    excludePaths:
        # `analyse`, not `analyseAndScan`: errors inside the vendored library
        # are not ours to fix, but its symbols must stay visible or every
        # `new PHPMailer(...)` in our own code becomes a false "class not found".
        analyse:
            - apps/status/lib
        analyseAndScan:
            # Written by the deploy workflow, absent in a checkout.
            - apps/status/version.php (?)
            - apps/status/config.php (?)
